WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.194 --> 00:00:01.906
- [Narrator] Meet Jenny Lee.

00:00:01.906 --> 00:00:04.514
- [Jenny] My name is Jenny, and
I lobbied for the dumpling,

00:00:04.514 --> 00:00:09.014
take-out box, fortune cookie,
and chopsticks emojis.

00:00:09.014 --> 00:00:10.533
- [Narrator] And this is
the story of how Jenny

00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:12.866
is getting up four new emoji.

00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:14.323
- [Jenny] Like it just felt
to me that there should be

00:00:14.323 --> 00:00:15.156
a dumpling emoji.

00:00:15.156 --> 00:00:15.989
And like, while we're at it,

00:00:15.989 --> 00:00:17.898
we should add the take-out
box, fortune cookie,

00:00:17.898 --> 00:00:19.121
and chopsticks.

00:00:19.121 --> 00:00:23.288
As of 2017, they are coming
to a keyboard near you.

00:00:24.853 --> 00:00:27.425
- [Narrator] Jenny was
on an emoji crusade.

00:00:27.425 --> 00:00:29.192
You see, in order to pitch an emoji,

00:00:29.192 --> 00:00:31.222
you have to infiltrate
this world-wide consortium

00:00:31.222 --> 00:00:32.541
named Unicode.

00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:34.580
- [Jenny] You get very quickly
to the Unicode consortium

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.878
which is a non-profit organization

00:00:36.878 --> 00:00:38.595
which basically oversees emoji.

00:00:38.595 --> 00:00:40.283
- [Narrator] They're
the people who control

00:00:40.283 --> 00:00:42.127
the alphabet of your emoji keyboard.

00:00:42.127 --> 00:00:44.023
- [Jenny] It has 11 full voting members.

00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:46.838
They pay $18,000 a year
to like have a full vote.

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:47.671
(siren blaring)

00:00:47.671 --> 00:00:49.106
- [Narrator] Unicode sets
the standard in parameters

00:00:49.106 --> 00:00:50.123
around emoji.

00:00:50.123 --> 00:00:53.494
You see, there are a set of
rules all emoji must abide by.

00:00:53.494 --> 00:00:55.074
- [Jenny] There are no brands,

00:00:55.074 --> 00:00:58.246
no deities, and no celebrities.

00:00:58.246 --> 00:00:59.730
And when we hear about quote Kimoji

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:01.772
those are not real accepted emoji.

00:01:01.772 --> 00:01:04.838
Those are sort of rogue emoji
that are really stickers.

00:01:04.838 --> 00:01:06.485
- [Narrator] So Jenny
started to attend meetings.

00:01:06.485 --> 00:01:08.828
For a swift $75, anyone
can attend the meeting

00:01:08.828 --> 00:01:10.717
as a non-voting member.

00:01:10.717 --> 00:01:13.556
- [Jenny] And you basically
like feel very welcome

00:01:13.556 --> 00:01:15.891
and so it feels a lot like
going to a new church.

00:01:15.891 --> 00:01:17.879
(Church chorale singing)

00:01:17.879 --> 00:01:18.712
- [Narrator] Back to the dumpling.

00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:21.439
- [Jenny] What I did was
write a whole proposal

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:23.378
about why the dumpling
deserved it's own emoji

00:01:23.378 --> 00:01:24.976
especially since it's so universal.

00:01:24.976 --> 00:01:26.809
And what's kind of cool is that not only

00:01:26.809 --> 00:01:28.047
is it a dumpling emoji,

00:01:28.047 --> 00:01:31.256
it can be a pierogi emoji,
or an empanada emoji.

00:01:31.256 --> 00:01:33.300
Got that passed as a candidate emoji

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.582
in January of this year.

00:01:34.582 --> 00:01:36.869
- [Narrator] Seems like a
lot of work for fun symbols.

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:38.287
- [Jenny] No, it was actually much faster

00:01:38.287 --> 00:01:39.120
than I thought.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.447
- [Narrator] Oh, okay.

00:01:40.447 --> 00:01:41.476
- [Jenny] I think it's really interesting

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:44.107
to watch the emoji evolve.

00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:45.354
- [Narrator] And that my friends

00:01:45.354 --> 00:01:47.235
is how an emoji is born.

00:01:47.235 --> 00:01:49.818
(upbeat music)

